 "summary": "The creator suggests that laziness is simply a habit of thinking about effort rather than the payoff. By focusing on the positive outcome instead of the hard work required, people can overcome inaction and accomplish difficult things.",
 "key_points": [
  "The creator claims laziness is a habit of thinking about the cost or effort of things instead of the payoff.",
  "The creator claims you can reverse laziness by developing a habit of thinking more about the delicious reward instead of how long it takes to get it.",
  "The creator compares this to childbirth, noting that people have second children because they focus on the awesome outcome of having a family rather than how hard the birth was."
 ],
 "content": "I wish I'd watched this before I gave up on myself. What if what if laziness is a habit of thinking about the cost of things or the effort instead of thinking about the payoff? I'm gonna say it again to get a few more heads to explode. What if laziness is nothing but a habit of thinking about the effort instead of thinking about the outcome? What if you could reverse laziness by simply developing a habit of thinking more about the uh let's say the delicious food that you would like to enjoy instead of how long it would take you to get up and go get it. But the but the real question is if you were to test this at home and try to see if you can think more about the good outcome and less about the work, would you get it done? Why isn't the people have a second child? You know, why does a woman who goes through this awful awful childbirth have a second child? Don't they always say the same thing? If I remembered how bad this was, I wouldn't do it again. Right? So the the not thinking about the effort is vital to the actually the survival of humanity. If we focused on how hard it was to have a baby, you just wouldn't do it. You do you do too little. But if you focus on how awesome it would be to have a family [unclear].",
